<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Slutsk</h1><p class="st-meta">1395 CE – 1592 CE (approx.) · capital Slutsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ruthenian duchy in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, ruled by the Olelkovich princes and famous for resisting Tatar raids.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1395 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-olgerdovich">Volodymyr Olgerdovich</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-kiev">Principality of Kiev</a> and gave him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slutsk">Slutsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapyl">Kapyl</a> in compensation. Volodymyr's descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> princes, held Slutsk as a hereditary duchy within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> for two centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> princes were among the greatest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> of the grand duchy, with their own court, troops and towns. In 1506 the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasia-of-slutsk">Anastasia of Slutsk</a> defended the town against a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> siege. The princes built churches and monasteries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slutsk">Slutsk</a> became a centre of Orthodox learning and crafts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The male line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> princes died out in 1592. Through the marriage of the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-olelkovich">Sophia Olelkovich</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-radziwill-1579-1620">Janusz Radziwill</a>, the duchy passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radziwill-family">Radziwill</a> family, who held it as a private estate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slutsk">Slutsk</a> later became famous for its woven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> sashes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-slutsk.jpg" alt="Duchy of Slutsk" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Divisions_of_Lithuania_1385_(Navahrudak_closeup).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1395 CE – 1592 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Slutsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Volodymyr Olgerdovich, Anastasia of Slutsk, Yury Olelkovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ruthenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Slutsk-Kapyl Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1494&amp;focus=duchy-of-slutsk" aria-label="Duchy of Slutsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5295 352 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Slutsk lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5615 608L5556 562"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5615 608h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5615 608h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Slutsk. <a href="/atlas?year=1494&amp;focus=duchy-of-slutsk">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

